1
00:00:00,302 --> 00:00:04,702
This is the Agent Run Reliability Desk,
an independent concept for Checkly.

2
00:00:04,803 --> 00:00:09,883
Its premise: a green HTTP status is not
proof that an agent did the right job.

3
00:00:09,984 --> 00:00:13,680
Eight synthetic fixtures are
seeded across two agent releases.

4
00:00:13,781 --> 00:00:19,498
You can also paste or upload your own JSON trace.
It is evaluated locally, with no telemetry.

5
00:00:19,598 --> 00:00:26,408
Running the validators applies deterministic rules,
not AI scores, to every step, tool call and source.

6
00:00:26,506 --> 00:00:32,945
The healthy refund passes both layers:
HTTP 200, and every declared invariant holds.

7
00:00:33,046 --> 00:00:39,522
Now the wrong tool target.
Transport passed with HTTP 200, but the task failed.

8
00:00:39,622 --> 00:00:46,252
The exact assertion shows the agent updated
ticket T-818 instead of T-881, at step s3.

9
00:00:46,352 --> 00:00:51,551
Against the baseline, six scenarios regressed,
so the release gate blocks the candidate.

10
00:00:51,649 --> 00:00:55,872
In the baseline,
the agent saw a stale status snapshot and abstained.

11
00:00:55,971 --> 00:01:00,543
That is a safe outcome,
so a human confirms it instead of it counting as a failure.

12
00:01:00,642 --> 00:01:08,938
An imported run with no steps and no declared invariants is not green either.
Nothing proves the job was done, so it goes to review.

13
00:01:09,037 --> 00:01:14,381
Finally, export a reproducible fixture with its fingerprint,
and a review memo for the hand-off.

14
00:01:14,481 --> 00:01:18,332
The alert preview stays concise,
and it is never sent anywhere.

15
00:01:18,433 --> 00:01:24,300
Independent concept by Ayo Ahmed.
Not affiliated with Checkly. All data is synthetic.
